Files: Getting Data Into Containers

A workload that needs a config file, a calibration table, or a model
has one path: the bytes become an OCI artifact in the registry, and
the spec carries only the digest. The journal stays pointers-only no
matter how large the files grow. This document covers the mechanism,
the blocking rule, and how to use it from the CLI, the SDK, or raw
HTTP.

The rule

Every persisted file becomes an artifact. There is no inline content
in specs, no size threshold with two behaviors, no second path. One
mechanism for 200 bytes and 5 gigabytes alike:

  local bytes ──push──▶ registry blob + manifest (concord-files repo)
                             │ syncs peer to peer like any image
  spec ──carries──▶ {path, digest, mode} ──journal──▶ every node
                             │
  assigned node ──pulls──▶ verifies digest ──writes──▶ container rootfs

Content addressing does three jobs at once. Dedup: ten workloads
sharing one calibration file store it once; re-pushes upload nothing.
Verification: addressing *is* the checksum, so tampered bytes fail the
pull, never the container. Replication: tagged repos sync through the
existing registry machinery with no new protocol.

Pushing: the artifact client

One implementation, shared three ways (sdk/fileart): SDK users call
it directly, the CLI calls it before submit, the daemon calls it at
start. It speaks four OCI calls and nothing else:

- HEAD blob: smart push. Bytes already stored skip the upload, so
  shared content costs two existence checks and zero bandwidth.
- POST uploads: monolithic blob store with the digest attached.
- PUT manifest: one manifest per file (exact bytes as the layer, an
  empty config, a tag from the blob hex so sync picks it up).
- GET blob: pull with hash verification on receipt.

  artifacts := fileart.NewClient("127.0.0.1:8444")
  digest, err := artifacts.Push(ctx, content)  // sha256:...; skipped if stored

The registry is always the loopback-local one. Bytes never cross the
network except through registry peer sync.

Blocking: the workload waits

A required blob that has not synced to the assigned node blocks the
workload: the start fails, the next tick retries, and the container
never runs without its files. This can last indefinitely on a
partition, by design. Starting half-provisioned is worse than waiting:
a planner without its map or a driver without its calibration does
damage, not work. The debug line per tick says which digest is
missing; concord workload inspect shows the pointers. Pulls stream
to disk with memory flat no matter the blob size: a 5 GB model never
sits in daemon RAM.

Paths and modes

Container paths must be absolute, clean, with no ... Anything else
is a 400 at submit: a relative path could land anywhere, an escape
could climb out of the container root. The materializer re-checks
defensively, because journal lines can be hand-written.

Modes default to 0600 (owner read-write). Pass an explicit mode for
the case that needs it, entrypoint scripts needing the execute bit
being the classic one. Anything above 0777 fails at submit.

  # One flag per file: container path, local path, optional mode
  concord workload run inference:latest --memory 2048 \
    --file /etc/model/config.yaml=./config.yaml \
    --file /entrypoint.sh=./entrypoint.sh:0755

From a Go program

Push first, then reference. The builder stays pure: it carries
pointers, never bytes.

  artifacts := fileart.NewClient("127.0.0.1:8444")
  digest, err := artifacts.Push(ctx, configBytes)
  if err != nil { /* push failed: submit nothing */ }

  w, err := sdk.NewWorkload().
    Image("inference:latest").
    MemoryMB(2048).
    QoS(sdk.QoSGuaranteed).
    File("/etc/model/config.yaml", digest, 0).
    Build()
  id, err := client.Submit(ctx, w)

From raw HTTP: push per the OCI distribution calls above, then submit
the spec JSON with a files array (path, digest, mode). The
endpoint shapes live in docs/ipc.

Size, secrets, garbage: documented, not solved

- Size. No cap. The journal is protected structurally (digests
  only), so registry growth is disk planning. The distribution
  doctrine still applies to large models: stage at the depot, pre-warm
  while charging, stagger rollouts (docs/deployment).
- Secrets. Blobs replicate in plaintext to every node. The mesh
  is trusted, so fleet credentials ride fine as-is. Anything with a
  narrower audience than the fleet (customer data, per-tenant keys)
  should be encrypted before the push and decrypted inside the
  container at start: Concord stays a byte store that never sees the
  key, and the application owns its secrecy end to end.
- Garbage. Stopped workloads leave their blobs; dedup softens it,
  cleanup is operator-owned like track rotation and journal archival.
  Registry-side collection is a later lever with numbers behind it.

The closed storage list

There are exactly three durable things: the journal (small state,
converges), the registry (blobs, content-addressed), the track file
(local history). Everything else is ephemeral inside the container
and dies with the workload.

